  "email": "hannah.lee@brightline.example",
  "reason": "KB-008 quick check: see if similar phishing messages were caught"
}

{
  "user_id": 12,
  "reason": "Precautionary check for any unusual sign-ins after phishing attempt"
}

{
  "outcome": "resolved",
  "reply_to_user": "Hi Hannah, good call not replying. That email is not from IT. Brightline IT will never ask for your password by email, chat, or phone, so treat any message like that as phishing. What to do now: don't click any links, reply, or forward it to anyone. Use the Report Phishing button in Outlook to send it to Security, then delete it. We checked recent sign-ins to your account and saw nothing unusual. Please let us know right away if any of these apply: you clicked a link or opened an attachment in that email, you typed your password anywhere, the email came from a real @brightline address, or coworkers got the same message. And never approve a Duo prompt you didn't start yourself. Thanks for flagging this!",
  "internal_note": "KB-008 followed. User (Hannah Lee, Finance/Payroll Specialist, user_id 12) got an email claiming to be from 'IT Support' asking her to reply with her password. She says she did not reply, and the ticket doesn't say she clicked anything or entered credentials, so KB-029 was not triggered. Checks: the account is active with 0 failed logins and sign-in is not blocked. The spam quarantine is empty, so the message was not caught by filtering and may be worth flagging to Security if others report it. Last 14 days of sign-ins: 9, all successful, all from New York on the usual corporate/residential IPs during normal hours, with no hosting-network sign-ins and no MFA denials. Observations: the sign-ins show mfa 'none' and must_change_password=true (password last set 2026-10-09), which fits a new or recently set up account. That may be why the lure about 'finishing account setup' was convincing. Consider confirming Duo enrollment. I did not run check_mfa_status, so enrollment is unconfirmed. User was told to report via the Outlook Report Phishing button and delete the email. No escalation for now: there's no sign of a compromise, the sender domain is unknown, and there's no report of multiple recipients. Escalate if the user says it came from an internal account or if other users report the same email.",
  "kb_articles": [
    "KB-008"
  ],
  "confidence": "high"
}
